#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""mesh-cron-catchup — run the daily cron lines whose slot fell into a powered-off node.

  mesh-cron-catchup [--dry] [--window HOURS] [--max N] [--json]
  mesh-cron-catchup --report            -> what each daily line's last slot did, no firing
  mesh-cron-catchup --test

# reflex-cadence: 9-59/15 * * * *
# reflex-state: $HOME/.mesh/cron-catchup.log
#
# The declared artifact is the LEDGER, not the cursor state. The state file only moves when a
# cursor advances — which for a daily line is once a day — so a watcher keyed on it would read a
# live tool as frozen for 23 hours out of 24. The ledger takes one `run` row per invocation
# whatever the verdict, so its mtime IS liveness and its content moves every pass. mesh-reflex-health
# derives its table from this header pair, so a reflex that declares neither is simply never
# watched: its roster is hand-enumerated otherwise, and this tool would have been invisible to it.

THE HOLE THIS CLOSES. This node power-cycles: measured 2026-08-25 over the previous 7 days,
**85 of 157 scheduled daily-line fires (54%) happened while the machine was off**, and 20 of the
24 daily lines missed 4 of their 4 fires. `mesh-job-apply` was one of them — its last real
application was 2026-08-21T07:36, four slots ago. cron has no memory: a slot that arrives while
the machine is off is not deferred, it is *gone*, and nothing downstream can tell that silence
apart from a tool that ran and declined. Every daily line here has the same hole; this is not a
job-lane fix.

WHAT DECIDES A MISS — the boot record, not the tool's own log. A tool's log cannot answer "did it
run", because a cron line's `>> log` appends nothing when the tool is silent, so mtime stays frozen
on a live run (the liveness-touch family). The axis used here is the one fact that is complete and
external to every tool: **was this machine up at the scheduled instant** (`journalctl
--list-boots`). Down at the slot -> the fire was lost -> catch it up. That is deterministic, and it
covers exactly the named fault.

WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* CLAIMED. Up-at-the-slot is treated as covered-by-cron, and this tool
does NOT re-fire it — a second hand firing a line cron already ran is a double-send, and one of
these lines mails employers under the operator's name. The other failure (machine up, cron dead)
is therefore OUT of this tool's reach by construction, and rather than guess, an up-at-slot line
whose own log has not been touched since the slot is published as `cron-suspect` — an advisory
naming, never an action. A `cron-suspect` that repeats is a finding for a hand, not for this loop.

TWO GRACE TERMS, both erring toward acting:
* `BOOT_GRACE` (120s) — a slot inside the first two minutes of a boot counts as MISSED, because
  cron is not necessarily up yet. Better a late second run (the callee's own cap guards it) than a
  slot silently attributed to a cron that had not started.
* `WINDOW` (default 26h) — how far back a lost slot is still worth running. Past that the world
  has moved and a stale digest is noise. Every slot older than the window is recorded as
  `expired`, never silently dropped.

THE LEDGER IS A DENOMINATOR, NOT A SCOREBOARD. `~/.mesh/cron-catchup.log` gets one `run` row per
invocation whatever happened, carrying how many slots were examined, so a silence in it is
downtime and not a decision. Fires get their own `fired` rows with the exit code. Cursor state is
`~/.mesh/cron-catchup.state`.

SEEDING. On the very first run the cursor is empty for every line, which would otherwise read as
"every slot ever is unfired". The first run seeds each line's cursor at the most recent slot and
fires nothing, printing `seeded`; catch-up begins with the next slot. That is one quiet run, and
it is announced.

ORDER AND BOUND. `--max` (default 4) fires at most N lines per invocation so a boot does not
stampede twenty tools at once, and the pending set is walked OLDEST-SLOT-FIRST, so the line that
has waited longest is the one that goes; the `9-59/15` cadence drains the rest within the hour
instead of a rotation away.

THE CALLEE DECLARES ITS OWN WORST CASE, and this hand reads it. Two optional headers, scanned
from the first BOUND_HEADER_LINES lines of the script a cron line launches:

    `# max-runtime: <seconds>`       the callee's own worst-case wall clock
    `# catchup-truncatable: <why>`   a fire cut short still leaves durable RESUMABLE progress

(Backticked on purpose. Written bare, those two lines would be read as a declaration BY THE READER
DOCUMENTED HERE, and this tool would mint a bound about itself out of its own manual — the gate for
that is a `--test` arm, and it went red on this very paragraph before the quoting was added.
[[a-source-text-gate-is-satisfied-by-its-own-explanatory-prose]])

Declared over FIRE_TIMEOUT and not truncatable -> this hand CANNOT deliver that catch-up: the fire
could only ever end in a kill at our bound, which is a failure we manufactured and then recorded
against a working tool. Such a slot is SKIPPED under its own ledger verb, nothing is spent (no
cursor claimed, no process run, the line's own cron still takes its next slot), and the board is
told once. Declared truncatable -> it IS fired and a cut-off is recorded as `truncated`, an
outcome and not a fault. A callee that declares nothing behaves exactly as before — an undeclared
bound is never guessed at, since 24 of the 25 daily lines here declare none.

THE LIVE CRONTAB IS THE SUBJECT, never `~/.mesh/reflexes.cron`. The two diverge — the desired file
has carried lines the live table did not for 27 days at a stretch — and only the live table fires,
so only the live table can miss.
"""
import argparse, calendar, fcntl, json, os, re, shutil, subprocess, sys, time

import signal

# SIGTERM MUST UNWIND, or every `finally` below is decorative. Python's DEFAULT SIGTERM disposition
# terminates the interpreter WITHOUT running finally blocks, TemporaryDirectory.__exit__ or atexit,
# so a correct-looking cleanup never executes. The signal is routine here, not hypothetical: GNU
# `timeout` without --foreground puts its child in its OWN PROCESS GROUP and signals the GROUP, so
# every timeout-wrapped call site -- mesh-doctor's hourly --test sweep, mesh-autowire's `timeout 30`
# -- delivers TERM to this process. That is how 950MB of stranded snapshots filled phaedra's /tmp on
# 2026-08-16 with a `finally: rmtree` sitting right there in the source (first fixed in mesh-spend,
# d2fd7c9b). sys.exit() from a handler raises SystemExit in the MAIN THREAD, which is what makes
# try/finally unwind. SIGKILL stays uncatchable -- that half is mesh-tmp-guard's sweep, never this.
for _sig in (signal.SIGTERM, signal.SIGHUP):
    signal.signal(_sig, lambda _s, _f: sys.exit(128 + _s))


HOME = os.path.expanduser("~")
MESH = os.path.join(HOME, ".mesh")
STATE = os.path.join(MESH, "cron-catchup.state")
LOCK = os.path.join(MESH, ".cron-catchup.lock")
LEDGER = os.path.join(MESH, "cron-catchup.log")
BOOT_GRACE = 120
WINDOW_H = float(os.environ.get("MESH_CATCHUP_WINDOW_H", "26"))
MAXFIRE = int(os.environ.get("MESH_CATCHUP_MAX", "4"))
# Long enough for the slowest daily line to finish. mesh-job-apply drives a real browser per
# application and its cap is now 20 (raised from 5 the same day), so a 900s bound — the first
# number here — would have TRUNCATED a full batch mid-send and recorded rc=124 on a line that was
# working. A timeout shorter than the callee's own worst case manufactures the failure it reports.
# THAT SENTENCE IS NO LONGER ONLY A SENTENCE. It sat here unenforced for three days while this bound
# cut mesh-test-forgery's 72000s-declared pass off at 2700s, 4 fires in a row, each recorded as a
# failure and each closing a slot; declared_bound() below is the check it needed.
FIRE_TIMEOUT = int(os.environ.get("MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT", "2700"))


def now_iso(t=None):
    return time.strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ", time.gmtime(t if t is not None else time.time()))


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cron fields
def parse_field(spec, lo, hi):
    """One cron field -> the set of values it fires on. Raises on anything not understood,
    because a field silently parsed as 'everything' would turn a weekly line into a daily one."""
    out = set()
    for part in spec.split(","):
        step = 1
        if "/" in part:
            part, s = part.split("/", 1)
            step = int(s)
            if step < 1:
                raise ValueError("step %r" % s)
        if part == "*":
            a, b = lo, hi
        elif "-" in part.lstrip("-"):
            a_s, b_s = part.split("-", 1)
            a, b = int(a_s), int(b_s)
        else:
            a = int(part)
            # `7/2` in a bare cron field means 7,9,11.. to the end of the range.
            b = a if step == 1 else hi
        if a < lo or b > hi or a > b:
            raise ValueError("range %r out of [%d,%d]" % (part, lo, hi))
        out.update(range(a, b + 1, step))
    if not out:
        raise ValueError("empty field %r" % spec)
    return out


class Line(object):
    def __init__(self, mins, hours, dom, mon, dow, cmd, raw):
        self.mins, self.hours, self.dom, self.mon, self.dow = mins, hours, dom, mon, dow
        self.cmd, self.raw = cmd, raw
        self.key = tool_of(cmd)

    def matches(self, g):
        if g.tm_min not in self.mins or g.tm_hour not in self.hours:
            return False
        if g.tm_mon not in self.mon:
            return False
        # cron's day-of-month / day-of-week are OR'd when both are restricted, AND'd otherwise.
        dowv = (g.tm_wday + 1) % 7  # python Mon=0 -> cron Sun=0
        dom_ok = g.tm_mday in self.dom
        dow_ok = dowv in self.dow or (dowv == 0 and 7 in self.dow)
        dom_all = len(self.dom) == 31
        dow_all = len(self.dow) >= 7
        if dom_all and dow_all:
            return True
        if not dom_all and not dow_all:
            return dom_ok or dow_ok
        return dom_ok and dow_ok


def tool_of(cmd):
    for tok in cmd.split():
        if "/" in tok and "mesh-" in tok:
            return tok.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    return cmd.split()[0].rsplit("/", 1)[-1]


# ------------------------------------------------- THE CALLEE'S OWN DECLARED BOUND
# "A timeout shorter than the callee's own worst case manufactures the failure it reports" was
# written in prose beside FIRE_TIMEOUT above and NOTHING CHECKED IT, so the pairing that sentence
# forbids ran for four days. mesh-test-forgery declares a 72000s wall-clock bound; this hand cut
# every fire off at 2700s -- rc=124 on 2026-08-25, 08-26 and 08-28 and a kill on 08-29, 4 of 4
# catch-up fires -- after which the slot is CLOSED with "a hand is the only re-runner". 45 minutes
# of wall clock burned per attempt to mint a guaranteed failure.
#
# THE FAILURE DIRECTION IS THE BAD ONE. A callee KILLED at our bound is indistinguishable in the
# ledger from a callee that ran and chose to exit 124, so the CLOSED verdict is minted against a
# tool that was working, and the hand it summons is sent to debug a health that was never sick.
# A principle written and unenforced is not a guard. So the bound is READ now, from the callee's
# own source -- never from a table in this file, which would be one more constant that outlives
# its reader:
#     # max-runtime: <seconds>          the callee's own worst-case wall clock
#     # catchup-truncatable: <why>      optional: a fire cut short still leaves durable, RESUMABLE
#                                       progress (a cursor advanced per item, not per pass)
# Declared bound over FIRE_TIMEOUT and NOT truncatable -> the slot is not fireable by this hand:
# SKIP it under its own verb, spend nothing, tell the board once. Never fire-and-close.
# Declared truncatable -> fire it, and a cut-off is recorded as `truncated`, which is an OUTCOME,
# not a fault: no FAILED alert, no closed-slot verdict against a tool that did exactly what it said
# it would do. [[a-vocabulary-with-two-states-reads-a-discipline-as-an-incident]]
BOUND_HEADER_LINES = 400


def script_of(cmd):
    """Absolute path of the script a cron line launches, or None. Same token rule as tool_of, so
    the bound is read from the file whose NAME this pass records -- a second, looser matcher here
    would let the two disagree about which tool a row is even about."""
    for tok in cmd.split():
        if "/" in tok and "mesh-" in tok:
            return os.path.expandvars(tok)
    first = os.path.expandvars(cmd.split()[0])
    return first if "/" in first else shutil.which(first)


def declared_bound(cmd, path=None):
    """(max_runtime_seconds | None, truncatable_reason | None) as the CALLEE declares them.

    Unreadable, absent, or undeclared all return None: a bound this hand cannot READ must not be
    invented, and the no-declaration case has to stay behaviourally identical to the tool before
    this existed -- 24 of the 25 daily lines here declare nothing, and a default guessed on their
    behalf would silence catch-ups that work today.
    """
    p = path if path is not None else script_of(cmd)
    if not p:
        return None, None
    sec, trunc = None, None
    try:
        with open(p, "r", errors="replace") as f:
            for i, raw in enumerate(f):
                if i >= BOUND_HEADER_LINES:
                    break
                line = raw.strip()
                if not line.startswith("#"):
                    continue
                m = re.match(r"#\s*max-runtime:\s*([0-9]+)\s*$", line)
                if m:
                    sec = int(m.group(1))
                m = re.match(r"#\s*catchup-truncatable:\s*(\S.*)$", line)
                if m:
                    trunc = m.group(1).strip()
    except (IOError, OSError):
        return None, None
    return sec, trunc


def outcome_of(rc, how, truncatable=None):
    """THE WORD for a fire's result -- one predicate, called by the ledger, the report and the
    board line, because a renderer that re-derives this classification drifts from the one the
    ledger recorded and two readers then disagree about the same row.

    rc alone cannot carry it. 124 is a cut-off when WE did the cutting and a real exit when the
    callee chose it; 2 is this mesh's DECLINE (organ absent / not applicable), which needs the
    opposite remedy from a fault -- nothing to re-run, unless the organ should have been there.
    """
    if rc == 0:
        return "ok"
    if how is None:
        # A row written before `how` was recorded. Most rcs still decide themselves -- 2 is the
        # decline whatever cut it, a negative is a signal -- but 124 is EXACTLY the pair this whole
        # change is about, and rc alone cannot say whether we cut it or the callee chose it. Say
        # UNRECORDED rather than pick one; a guess here would re-mint the verdict against a working
        # tool from the other side.
        if rc == 124:
            return "outcome-unrecorded"
        if rc < 0:
            return "killed"
        return "declined" if rc == 2 else "failed"
    if how == "timeout":
        return "truncated" if truncatable else "cut-off"
    if how.startswith("exec:"):
        return "exec-error"
    if rc < 0:
        return "killed"
    if rc == 2:
        return "declined"
    return "failed"


def read_crontab(text=None):
    """The LIVE table, because only the live table fires. Daily-ish == both minute and hour are
    enumerated, i.e. the line fires a small fixed number of times a day; those are the ones a
    power-off can eat whole. An hourly-or-finer line is re-fired by its own next tick."""
    if text is None:
        p = subprocess.run(["crontab", "-l"], capture_output=True, text=True)
        text = p.stdout if p.returncode == 0 else ""
    out = []
    for raw in text.splitlines():
        s = raw.strip()
        if not s or s.startswith("#") or "=" in s.split()[0]:
            continue
        f = s.split(None, 5)
        if len(f) < 6:
            continue
        m, h = f[0], f[1]
        if "*" in m or "*" in h:
            continue
        try:
            out.append(Line(parse_field(f[0], 0, 59), parse_field(f[1], 0, 23),
                            parse_field(f[2], 1, 31), parse_field(f[3], 1, 12),
                            parse_field(f[4], 0, 7), f[5], s))
        except ValueError:
            continue
    return out


def slots(line, t_from, t_to):
    """Every instant in (t_from, t_to] this line fires on, ascending. Minute resolution."""
    out = []
    t = int(t_to) // 60 * 60
    while t > t_from:
        if line.matches(time.gmtime(t)):
            out.append(t)
        t -= 60
    return sorted(out)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- up-windows
def boot_windows(raw=None):
    """(start,end) per boot from the journal. This is the whole basis of the verdict, so a failure
    to read it must NOT degrade into 'the node was up' — that would silently answer 'covered' for
    every slot and turn this tool into a no-op that looks healthy. It returns None, and the caller
    renders UNKNOWN and fires nothing."""
    if raw is None:
        p = subprocess.run(["journalctl", "--list-boots", "-o", "json"],
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
        if p.returncode != 0 or not p.stdout.strip():
            return None
        raw = p.stdout
    try:
        bs = json.loads(raw)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    w = []
    for b in bs:
        try:
            w.append((b["first_entry"] / 1e6, b["last_entry"] / 1e6))
        except (KeyError, TypeError):
            return None
    return w or None


def up_at(t, windows):
    return any(s + BOOT_GRACE <= t <= e for s, e in windows)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- state
def load_state():
    try:
        with open(STATE) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except (IOError, ValueError):
        return {}


def save_state(st):
    tmp = STATE + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(st, f, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
    os.replace(tmp, STATE)


def ledger(kind, **kv):
    row = "%s\t%s\t%s\n" % (now_iso(), kind,
                            "\t".join("%s=%s" % (k, v) for k, v in sorted(kv.items())))
    try:
        with open(LEDGER, "a") as f:
            f.write(row)
    except IOError:
        pass


# A FAILED FIRE MUST HAVE A READER. [[a-watcher-whose-only-actuator-is-attention]]
# fire() returns an rc, main() wrote it into the state and the ledger, and NOTHING read it. There is
# no board post in this file and fire() sends stdout and stderr to DEVNULL, so on 2026-08-26 a whole
# day's job applications vanished (rc=-15 at the 07:23 slot, 7 sent instead of 20 against the
# operator's 5 September deadline) and the entire trace of it was ONE line in ~/.mesh/cron-catchup.log.
#
# THE REMEDY IS LOUDNESS, NOT A RETRY. The cursor is claimed BEFORE the fire on purpose — at-most-once,
# because one of these lines mails employers under the operator's name, and re-opening a killed slot
# buys a double send (measured 2026-08-25: mesh-log-attest fired TWICE when the cursor was saved after).
# So the slot stays closed and this tool can never re-run it. That is exactly why it must SAY so: a
# HAND is the only re-runner, and a hand that is never told is not a re-runner at all.
def alert_failed_fire(tool, slot, rc, how):
    """Raise the board line a non-zero fire owes. Returns 1 posted / 0 refused / -1 could not."""
    # rc<0 is a SIGNAL: the child was KILLED by something outside it, which is a different diagnosis
    # from the line failing on its own, and collapsing the two would send a human to read the wrong
    # log. [[a-collapsed-reason-makes-blind-and-quiet-identical]]
    if rc < 0:
        why = ("killed by signal %d — something outside the line stopped it; suspect a second writer "
               "on a resource the line holds alone" % -rc)
    elif how == "timeout":
        why = "exceeded FIRE_TIMEOUT=%ds and was cut off" % FIRE_TIMEOUT
    elif how.startswith("exec:"):
        why = "could not be executed at all (%s)" % how[5:]
    elif rc == 2:
        # 2 is this mesh's DECLARED n/a (organ absent / not applicable here), not a fault. It still
        # gets an alert — a catch-up fire only happens for a slot that was actually MISSED, so these
        # are rare by construction and silence is the failure direction we are closing — but it is
        # TAGGED apart so a reader triages it without digging. A vocabulary with one word for "broke"
        # and "declined" reads a discipline as an incident.
        # [[a-vocabulary-with-two-states-reads-a-discipline-as-an-incident]]
        why = ("exited 2, which is this mesh's n/a convention (organ absent / not applicable) — "
               "likely a DECLINE rather than a fault, but the slot is spent either way")
    else:
        why = "exited %d on its own" % rc
    msg = ("[fyi] mesh-cron-catchup: CATCH-UP FIRE FAILED — %s, slot %s, rc=%s (%s). %s. "
           "THE SLOT IS CLOSED AND WILL NOT BE RETRIED: the cursor is claimed before the fire so this "
           "lane is at-most-once by design, which means this tool can never re-run it and a HAND is "
           "the only re-runner. Whatever that slot was meant to do did not happen." % (tool, slot, rc, how, why))
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["mesh-chat", msg], timeout=20,
                           stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        return 1 if r.returncode == 0 else -1
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        # An alerter that fails silently rebuilds the very defect it exists to close, one ring out.
        # The caller writes this outcome into the ledger so a dead alerter is visible as a dead
        # alerter and never as a quiet day. [[a-gates-verdict-with-no-reader]]
        return -1


def alert_bound_mismatch(tool, slot, declared, st):
    """The board line an UN-FIREABLE slot owes. Returns 1 posted / 0 already told / -1 could not.

    This is the quiet half of the fault. Firing was at least visible; declining to fire is a
    SILENCE unless it is said out loud, and the reader has to be told the thing that is NOT true
    of every other non-zero row here: the slot was NOT spent. Nothing was claimed, nothing ran, and
    the line's own cron will take its next scheduled slot normally -- this hand simply cannot
    deliver a catch-up whose callee needs longer than this hand will wait.

    Throttled to ONE post per (tool, slot). The slot stays `missed` until it ages out of the
    window, so every 15-minute pass re-derives it; an unthrottled post would put ~100 identical
    lines on the board per slot and the finding would drown in its own repetition.
    """
    e = st.setdefault(tool, {})
    if e.get("bound_alert_slot") == slot:
        return 0
    msg = ("[fyi] mesh-cron-catchup: CATCH-UP SKIPPED (not failed) — %s, slot %s. The line declares "
           "its own worst case as max-runtime=%ss and this hand waits FIRE_TIMEOUT=%ss, so firing it "
           "could only ever end in a kill at our bound — a manufactured failure recorded against a "
           "tool that was working. NOTHING WAS SPENT: no cursor was claimed, no process ran, and the "
           "line's own cron takes its next scheduled slot as usual; only the CATCH-UP for this one "
           "slot is forgone. To make it catch-uppable either raise MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT past %ss "
           "(which wedges this lane for that long — the pass holds a single lock) or, if a fire cut "
           "short still leaves durable resumable progress, declare `# catchup-truncatable: <why>` in "
           "the callee." % (tool, slot, declared, FIRE_TIMEOUT, declared))
    try:
        r = subprocess.run(["mesh-chat", msg], timeout=20,
                           stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        if r.returncode != 0:
            return -1
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return -1
    e["bound_alert_slot"] = slot
    return 1


def failed_slots(st):
    """The lines whose fire RETURNED non-zero — the reader that did not exist.

    A returned rc is an INT, while the pass-died-mid-fire marker is the literal string "in-flight";
    the only reader this tool had matched that string, so the one case with a real failed outcome
    fell through it entirely and a lost daily batch rendered as a quiet day.

    This is the DECIDING PREDICATE and it has exactly one definition: the renderer and the test both
    call it, because a test that re-implements the predicate it is checking passes while the code it
    was written for is broken. [[a-lint-that-tests-a-different-predicate-than-the-parser-is-blind]]
    in-flight and failed stay SEPARATE facts: the first is "we do not know whether it ran", the
    second is "it ran and did not work", and one word for both would lose the distinction.
    """
    out = []
    for k, v in st.items():
        rc = v.get("last_rc")
        # bool is a subclass of int in Python; an isinstance check alone would admit a True that
        # never came from a process. Cheap to exclude, and it costs nothing when it never happens.
        if isinstance(rc, int) and not isinstance(rc, bool) and rc != 0:
            # A fire the callee DECLARED cuttable, cut at our bound, is not a failed slot: it ran,
            # it did bounded durable work, and it said in its own source that this is fine. Reading
            # it as a failure is the defect this predicate exists to avoid, one ring in -- and the
            # word comes from the recorded outcome, never re-derived, so the renderer cannot
            # disagree with the ledger row that was written at the time.
            if v.get("last_outcome") == "truncated":
                continue
            out.append((k, v))
    return sorted(out)


def log_mtime(cmd):
    """The `>> path` a cron line redirects into, if any — advisory only."""
    toks = cmd.split()
    for i, t in enumerate(toks):
        if t == ">>" and i + 1 < len(toks):
            p = os.path.expandvars(toks[i + 1])
            try:
                return os.path.getmtime(p)
            except OSError:
                return None
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the pass
def examine(lines, windows, st, t_now):
    """Classify each daily line's most recent slot. Pure: returns rows, mutates nothing."""
    horizon = t_now - WINDOW_H * 3600
    rows = []
    for ln in lines:
        ss = slots(ln, t_now - max(WINDOW_H, 24 * 8) * 3600, t_now)
        if not ss:
            rows.append({"key": ln.key, "verdict": "no-slot", "slot": None, "line": ln})
            continue
        # An unseeded line starts at the window's edge, NOT at zero and NOT at "now". Zero would
        # fire years of dead slots; "now" (the first design here) threw away the very backlog the
        # tool exists for — on this node's first pass that was 20 lines that had genuinely missed
        # their slot hours earlier. The window is already the staleness bound; a second, quieter
        # bound stacked on top of it just makes the first run a no-op that looks healthy.
        cur = st.get(ln.key, {}).get("cursor", t_now - WINDOW_H * 3600)
        pending = [s for s in ss if s > cur]
        if not pending:
            rows.append({"key": ln.key, "verdict": "covered", "slot": ss[-1], "line": ln})
            continue
        s = pending[0]  # oldest unresolved first — never a full rotation away
        # A backlog older than the window is all equally dead; skip it whole rather than one
        # slot per tick, or a week off-grid takes a week of ticks to walk past.
        stale = [x for x in pending if x < horizon]
        skip_to = stale[-1] if stale else None
        if s < horizon:
            v = "expired"
        elif up_at(s, windows):
            lm = log_mtime(ln.cmd)
            v = "cron-suspect" if (lm is not None and lm < s) else "covered"
        else:
            v = "missed"
        rows.append({"key": ln.key, "verdict": v, "slot": s, "line": ln,
                     "pending": len(pending),
                     "skip_to": skip_to if v == "expired" else None})
    return rows


def _killpg(p):
    """TERM the fire's process GROUP, escalate to KILL, and never raise.

    start_new_session below gives each fire its own group, which is the whole point — but it also
    means `p.kill()` would reap the `sh` and ORPHAN every grandchild it spawned (a browser driver,
    a python batch). Signal the group, exactly as `timeout` does.
    """
    for sig, grace in ((signal.SIGTERM, 10.0), (signal.SIGKILL, 2.0)):
        try:
            os.killpg(p.pid, sig)
        except OSError:
            return
        try:
            p.wait(timeout=grace)
            return
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            continue


def fire(ln, dry):
    """Run the line the way cron would: /bin/sh, cron's own PATH, no login shell. A richer env
    here would let a line pass in catch-up that fails at its real slot.

    THE FIRE GETS ITS OWN SESSION, AND THAT IS THE LOAD-BEARING HALF. The cursor is claimed BEFORE
    the fire (at-most-once, see main()), so a fire that dies has SPENT a slot nothing will re-run.
    Until 2026-08-26 the child was left in the CALLER'S process group, which means every ordinary
    group-directed signal aimed at the pass — an operator Ctrl-C in a pane, an agent tool-call
    timeout, a `timeout` without --foreground anywhere up the chain — landed on the fire too.
    Measured that day: slot 2026-08-26T07:23:00Z, tool mesh-job-apply, rc=-15 after 46s against a
    normal pass of 455-980s (`~/.mesh/job-apply.log` run-end rc=143 how=sys.exit); the slot was
    recorded as spent and the day sent 7 applications instead of 20 against the operator's
    5 September deadline. The pass itself is disposable — it runs every 15 minutes. The fire is
    NOT: it holds the only claim on an irreversible slot. So the two must not share a fate.
    [[a-name-matching-stop-kills-your-other-selves]] one ring out: the signal here is not even
    name-matched, it is aimed at a group the fire had no business being in.

    This does NOT re-open a killed slot, and must not: re-opening buys a double send on a lane that
    mails employers under the operator's name (measured 2026-08-25, mesh-log-attest fired twice).
    The remedy is that the fire stops being killable BY THE PASS, not that a kill stops counting.
    """
    if dry:
        return 0, "dry"
    env = {"HOME": HOME, "PATH": "/usr/bin:/bin", "SHELL": "/bin/sh",
           "LOGNAME": os.environ.get("LOGNAME", ""), "USER": os.environ.get("USER", ""),
           "LANG": os.environ.get("LANG", "C.UTF-8"),
           "MESH_CRON_CATCHUP": "1"}
    try:
        p = subprocess.Popen(["/bin/sh", "-c", os.path.expandvars(ln.cmd)],
                             env=env, stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                             start_new_session=True)
    except OSError as e:
        return 127, "exec:%s" % e
    try:
        return p.wait(timeout=FIRE_TIMEOUT), "ran"
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        # FIRE_TIMEOUT still binds — the detachment buys the fire independence from the pass's
        # fate, never immortality. A bound whose enforcement the change quietly dropped would be
        # the worse defect of the two.
        _killpg(p)
        return 124, "timeout"


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    ap.add_argument("--dry", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--report", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--window", type=float)
    ap.add_argument("--max", type=int)
    ap.add_argument("--test", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--boot-windows", action="store_true",
                    help="print the boot record as JSON [[start,end],...] epoch seconds and exit")
    a = ap.parse_args()
    if a.test:
        return selftest()
    if a.boot_windows:
        # THE boot-record entry point. This tool already owns the only parser of
        # `journalctl --list-boots` in the mesh, and a second private copy of it in another tool
        # would rot independently — so the read is exposed here instead of duplicated. Same
        # honest-blindness contract as the verdict path: unreadable is rc=2, never an empty
        # "the node was up" set that a caller would sum to zero downtime.
        w = boot_windows()
        if w is None:
            print("UNKNOWN: boot record unreadable (journalctl --list-boots)", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        print(json.dumps([[s, e] for s, e in w]))
        return 0
    global WINDOW_H, MAXFIRE
    if a.window:
        WINDOW_H = a.window
    if a.max is not None:
        MAXFIRE = a.max

    # A pass can spend --max * 900s firing, which is longer than the cadence between passes. Two
    # overlapping passes would each read the cursor before either wrote it and fire the same slot
    # twice — and one of these lines mails employers. Non-blocking: a pass that finds the lock held
    # exits quietly, because the holder is already doing this pass's work.
    lockf = open(LOCK, "w")
    try:
        fcntl.flock(lockf, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    except (IOError, OSError):
        ledger("run", examined=0, fired=0, verdict="skipped", reason="pass-already-running")
        print("skipped: another pass holds %s" % LOCK)
        return 0

    t_now = time.time()
    lines = read_crontab()
    windows = boot_windows()
    if windows is None:
        # The one axis this tool reasons on is unreadable. Say so and fire nothing.
        ledger("run", examined=len(lines), fired=0, verdict="UNKNOWN",
               reason="boot-record-unreadable")
        print("UNKNOWN: boot record unreadable (journalctl --list-boots) — "
              "nothing fired, %d daily line(s) unexamined" % len(lines))
        return 2

    st = load_state()
    rows = examine(lines, windows, st, t_now)
    fired = []
    # oldest missed slot first
    todo = sorted([r for r in rows if r["verdict"] == "missed"], key=lambda r: r["slot"])

    # READ THE CALLEE'S OWN BOUND BEFORE SPENDING ITS SLOT. A line whose declared worst case is
    # longer than FIRE_TIMEOUT cannot be caught up by this hand at all: the fire can only end in a
    # kill at our bound, and the cursor claimed before it turns that into a CLOSED slot no one will
    # re-run. Un-fireable rows leave the fire budget ENTIRELY -- they must not consume a MAXFIRE
    # slot either, or one un-fireable line at the head of the oldest-first queue starves every line
    # behind it for as long as its slot stays inside the window.
    skipped, fireable = [], []
    for r in todo:
        r["declared"], r["truncatable"] = declared_bound(r["line"].cmd)
        if r["declared"] is not None and r["declared"] > FIRE_TIMEOUT and not r["truncatable"]:
            r["verdict"] = "bound-mismatch"
            skipped.append(r)
        else:
            fireable.append(r)
    for r in skipped:
        alerted = "n/a"
        if not (a.dry or a.report):
            alerted = {1: "yes", 0: "already-told", -1: "FAILED"}[
                alert_bound_mismatch(r["key"], now_iso(r["slot"]), r["declared"], st)]
            save_state(st)
        # Its OWN verb. `fired` with an rc would say a process ran; nothing ran, and the cursor is
        # untouched, so this row is not a member of the fire census at all.
        ledger("skipped-bound", tool=r["key"], slot=now_iso(r["slot"]),
               declared=r["declared"], fire_timeout=FIRE_TIMEOUT, alerted=alerted,
               dry=int(bool(a.dry or a.report)))

    for r in fireable[:MAXFIRE]:
        # THE CURSOR IS WRITTEN BEFORE THE FIRE, NOT AFTER THE PASS. Measured 2026-08-25: the first
        # version saved state once, at the end of the pass, and mesh-log-attest fired TWICE for the
        # same 04:17 slot — the pass was killed between the two, so nothing it had already done was
        # on disk and the next pass re-derived the identical work list. On a node that power-cycles
        # (the very fault this tool exists for) being killed mid-pass is the ordinary case, not an
        # edge, and one of these lines mails employers under the operator's name. Claiming the slot
        # first means a crash costs at most ONE lost catch-up; claiming it last means a crash
        # repeats every send the pass had made.
        if not (a.dry or a.report):
            e = st.setdefault(r["key"], {})
            e["cursor"] = r["slot"]
            e["last_fire"] = now_iso(t_now)
            e["last_rc"] = "in-flight"
            save_state(st)
        rc, how = fire(r["line"], a.dry or a.report)
        r["rc"], r["how"] = rc, how
        r["outcome"] = outcome = outcome_of(rc, how, r.get("truncatable"))
        fired.append(r)
        alerted = "n/a"
        if not (a.dry or a.report):
            st[r["key"]]["last_rc"] = rc
            # The WORD travels with the rc into the state, so every later reader renders the same
            # verdict this pass reached. Re-deriving it downstream would need `how` and the callee's
            # declaration again, and a reader that has neither prints "failed" for a cut-off the
            # callee had licensed.
            st[r["key"]]["last_how"] = how
            st[r["key"]]["last_outcome"] = outcome
            save_state(st)
            if outcome not in ("ok", "truncated"):
                # The state write comes FIRST: the record of what happened must survive even if the
                # board is unreachable. The alert is the second-best artifact, never the only one.
                alerted = {1: "yes", -1: "FAILED"}[alert_failed_fire(r["key"], now_iso(r["slot"]), rc, how)]
        ledger("fired", tool=r["key"], slot=now_iso(r["slot"]), rc=rc, how=how,
               outcome=outcome, alerted=alerted, dry=int(bool(a.dry or a.report)))
    if not (a.dry or a.report):
        for r in rows:
            if r["verdict"] in ("expired", "covered", "cron-suspect") and r["slot"]:
                st.setdefault(r["key"], {})["cursor"] = r.get("skip_to") or r["slot"]
                st[r["key"]]["last_verdict"] = r["verdict"]
        save_state(st)

    counts = {}
    for r in rows:
        counts[r["verdict"]] = counts.get(r["verdict"], 0) + 1
    ledger("run", examined=len(rows), fired=len([f for f in fired if f.get("how") == "ran"]),
           dry=int(bool(a.dry or a.report)),
           **{("n_" + k): v for k, v in counts.items()})

    if a.json:
        print(json.dumps([{k: (now_iso(v) if k == "slot" and v else v)
                           for k, v in r.items() if k != "line"} for r in rows],
                         ensure_ascii=False, indent=1))
        return 0
    print("mesh-cron-catchup %s  window=%gh max=%d  daily-lines=%d" %
          (now_iso(t_now), WINDOW_H, MAXFIRE, len(rows)))
    print("  " + "  ".join("%s=%d" % (k, v) for k, v in sorted(counts.items())))
    for r in sorted(rows, key=lambda r: (r["verdict"], r["key"])):
        if r["verdict"] == "covered":
            continue
        print("  %-14s %-24s slot=%s%s" % (
            r["verdict"], r["key"], now_iso(r["slot"]) if r["slot"] else "n/a",
            ("  rc=%s (%s)" % (r["rc"], r["how"])) if "rc" in r else ""))
    left = len(fireable) - len(fired)
    if left > 0:
        print("  %d missed slot(s) still pending — next pass takes the oldest" % left)
    stuck = sorted(k for k, v in st.items() if v.get("last_rc") == "in-flight")
    if stuck:
        print("  in-flight (a pass died mid-fire, slot already claimed): " + ", ".join(stuck))
    for k, v in failed_slots(st):
        # A STAND-DOWN AND A FAILURE NEED TWO WORDS WITH OPPOSITE REMEDIES. Six lines on this node
        # read as CLOSED-as-failed on 2026-08-29 and four of them had exited 2 -- the mesh's own n/a
        # convention -- i.e. they declined honestly and were filed as broken. A hand sent to re-run
        # a tool that correctly stood down is a hand spent on nothing, and worse, it teaches the
        # reader to discount the word that also names the real failures.
        # Rows written before the outcome word existed carry rc alone. Most rcs still decide
        # themselves; the one that cannot is 124, and that one renders UNRECORDED rather than
        # being guessed from evidence that does not contain the answer.
        word = v.get("last_outcome") or outcome_of(v["last_rc"], v.get("last_how"))
        if word == "declined":
            print("  declined (fire exited 2 at %s — this mesh's n/a convention: organ absent or the "
                  "lane does not apply here. The slot IS spent and this tool cannot re-run it, but "
                  "there is nothing to re-run unless that organ should have been present): %s"
                  % (v.get("last_fire", "?"), k))
        else:
            print("  failed (fire returned rc=%s (%s) at %s; slot CLOSED, a hand is the only "
                  "re-runner): %s" % (v["last_rc"], word, v.get("last_fire", "?"), k))
    for k, v in sorted(st.items()):
        if v.get("last_outcome") == "truncated":
            print("  truncated (fire cut at FIRE_TIMEOUT=%ds at %s — the callee DECLARES this safe "
                  "(catchup-truncatable), so its bounded work stands and no hand is owed): %s"
                  % (FIRE_TIMEOUT, v.get("last_fire", "?"), k))
    if any(r["verdict"] == "bound-mismatch" for r in rows):
        print("  bound-mismatch: the line's own `# max-runtime:` header exceeds FIRE_TIMEOUT=%ds, so "
              "a fire could only end in a kill at our bound. NOT fired and NOTHING SPENT — the "
              "cursor is untouched and the line's own cron still takes its next slot." % FIRE_TIMEOUT)
    if any(r["verdict"] == "cron-suspect" for r in rows):
        print("  cron-suspect: node WAS up at the slot but the line's own log is older than it. "
              "Not fired (a double-fire is worse); a repeat is a finding for a hand.")
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- test
def selftest():
    fails = []

    def check(name, ok):
        check.n += 1
        print(("  ok   " if ok else "  FAIL ") + name)
        if not ok:
            fails.append(name)
    check.n = 0

    # --- field parser
    check("*/15 expands to four minutes", parse_field("*/15", 0, 59) == {0, 15, 30, 45})
    check("9-59/15 keeps its phase", parse_field("9-59/15", 0, 59) == {9, 24, 39, 54})
    check("a bare list is exactly the list", parse_field("9,18", 0, 23) == {9, 18})
    bad = False
    try:
        parse_field("99", 0, 59)
    except ValueError:
        bad = True
    check("an out-of-range field RAISES, never silently becomes everything", bad)

    # --- selection: only lines a power-off can eat whole
    tab = ("*/10 * * * * /home/x/.local/bin/mesh-algedonic >> /tmp/a.log 2>&1\n"
           "23 7 * * * /home/x/.local/bin/mesh-job-apply --top 5 --tg >> /tmp/b.log 2>&1\n"
           "0 9 * * 1 /home/x/.local/bin/mesh-board-weekly >> /tmp/c.log 2>&1\n"
           "37 * * * * /home/x/.local/bin/mesh-chat-review >> /tmp/d.log 2>&1\n"
           "# 41 6 * * * /home/x/.local/bin/mesh-commented >> /tmp/e.log 2>&1\n")
    ls = read_crontab(tab)
    keys = sorted(l.key for l in ls)
    check("an hourly-or-finer line is NOT a subject (its own next tick re-fires it)",
          "mesh-algedonic" not in keys and "mesh-chat-review" not in keys)
    check("a commented line is not a subject", "mesh-commented" not in keys)
    check("daily and weekly lines ARE subjects", keys == ["mesh-board-weekly", "mesh-job-apply"])

    # --- weekly really is weekly (the dow field must not be widened by a sloppy parse)
    wk = [l for l in ls if l.key == "mesh-board-weekly"][0]
    mon = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 24, 9, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))   # a Monday
    tue = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 9, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    check("a weekly line fires on its day", wk.matches(time.gmtime(mon)))
    check("a weekly line does NOT fire on the other six", not wk.matches(time.gmtime(tue)))

    # --- the verdict axis, driven against a fabricated boot record
    ja = [l for l in ls if l.key == "mesh-job-apply"][0]
    t_now = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 10, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    slot = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 7, 23, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    check("the last slot before now is found", slots(ja, t_now - 86400, t_now)[-1] == slot)

    down = [(calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 9, 57, 0, 0, 0, 0)), t_now)]      # booted after the slot
    upw = [(calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)), t_now)]        # up across the slot
    seeded = {"mesh-job-apply": {"cursor": slot - 86400}}

    r = [x for x in examine([ja], down, seeded, t_now) if x["key"] == "mesh-job-apply"][0]
    check("a slot inside a power-off is MISSED", r["verdict"] == "missed")
    r = [x for x in examine([ja], upw, seeded, t_now) if x["key"] == "mesh-job-apply"][0]
    check("the SAME slot with the node up is NOT missed (no double-fire)",
          r["verdict"] in ("covered", "cron-suspect"))
    # the mutation that matters: if up_at ignored its windows, the two arms above would agree.
    check("the two arms actually disagree — the boot record is what decides",
          examine([ja], down, seeded, t_now)[0]["verdict"]
          != examine([ja], upw, seeded, t_now)[0]["verdict"])

    boot_at_slot = [(slot - 30, t_now)]   # machine came up 30s before the slot
    r = examine([ja], boot_at_slot, seeded, t_now)[0]
    check("a slot inside the boot grace counts as MISSED, not silently covered",
          r["verdict"] == "missed")

    r = examine([ja], down, {}, t_now)[0]
    check("an unseeded line DOES catch up its in-window miss (the point of the tool)",
          r["verdict"] == "missed" and r["slot"] == slot)
    far = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 10, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    saved = globals()["WINDOW_H"]
    globals()["WINDOW_H"] = 1.0
    r = examine([ja], down, {}, far)[0]
    globals()["WINDOW_H"] = saved
    check("an unseeded line never reaches back past the window",
          r["verdict"] != "missed")

    old = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 20, 7, 23, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    r = examine([ja], down, {"mesh-job-apply": {"cursor": old - 60}},
                calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 10, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)))[0]
    check("a slot past the window is EXPIRED, and says so rather than vanishing",
          r["verdict"] == "expired")

    # --- the honest-blindness arm: an unreadable boot record must not read as 'node was up'
    check("an unparseable boot record returns None, never an empty 'was up' set",
          boot_windows("not json") is None and boot_windows("[]") is None)
    check("a boot record missing its fields returns None",
          boot_windows('[{"index":0}]') is None)

    # --- oldest-first ordering
    two = read_crontab("5 7 * * * /b/mesh-a >> /tmp/a.log 2>&1\n"
                       "5 9 * * * /b/mesh-b >> /tmp/b.log 2>&1\n")
    midnight = calendar.timegm((2026, 8, 25, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0))
    st2 = {"mesh-a": {"cursor": midnight}, "mesh-b": {"cursor": midnight}}
    rr = [x for x in examine(two, down, st2, t_now) if x["verdict"] == "missed"]
    order = [x["key"] for x in sorted(rr, key=lambda x: x["slot"])]
    check("both same-day misses are seen", sorted(x["key"] for x in rr) == ["mesh-a", "mesh-b"])
    check("the oldest missed slot is fired first", order[:1] == ["mesh-a"])

    # --- the cursor must be on disk BEFORE the fire, or a killed pass repeats its sends
    import tempfile
    global STATE, FIRE_TIMEOUT
    keep = STATE
    seen = []
    _sd = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-test-")
    try:
        STATE = os.path.join(_sd, "s.json")
        st3 = {}
        st3.setdefault("mesh-x", {})["cursor"] = 123
        save_state(st3)
        # emulate the ordering main() uses: claim, then fire, and read what disk held at fire time
        st4 = load_state()
        st4["mesh-x"]["cursor"] = 456
        save_state(st4)
        seen.append(load_state()["mesh-x"]["cursor"])
    finally:
        STATE = keep
        shutil.rmtree(_sd, ignore_errors=True)
    check("save_state is durable and read-back before the next step sees it", seen == [456])
    src = open(os.path.abspath(__file__)).read()
    fire_at = src.index("rc, how = fire(r[\"line\"]")
    claim_at = src.index("e[\"last_rc\"] = \"in-flight\"")
    check("the slot is claimed on disk ABOVE the fire call, not below it", claim_at < fire_at)

    # --- THE READER A FAILED FIRE DID NOT HAVE ------------------------------------------------
    # 2026-08-26: rc=-15 at the 07:23 mesh-job-apply slot, 7 applications sent instead of 20 against
    # the operator's 5 September deadline, and the whole trace was one ledger line nobody reads.
    # Every arm below asserts the same thing from a different side: a fire that failed must reach a
    # HUMAN, and it must say that nothing will retry it. [[a-watcher-whose-only-actuator-is-attention]]
    _ad = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-alert-")
    _keep_path = os.environ.get("PATH", "")
    try:
        rec = os.path.join(_ad, "said")
        with open(os.path.join(_ad, "mesh-chat"), "w") as f:
            f.write('#!/bin/sh\nprintf "%s\\n" "$1" >> ' + rec + '\nexit 0\n')
        os.chmod(os.path.join(_ad, "mesh-chat"), 0o755)
        os.environ["PATH"] = _ad + os.pathsep + _keep_path

        def said(rc, how):
            open(rec, "w").close()
            got = alert_failed_fire("mesh-job-apply", "2026-08-26T07:23:00Z", rc, how)
            # The stub WINNING the PATH race is itself an assertion: alert_failed_fire must not set
            # its own env, or the stub would be behind the real tool and this suite would be
            # measuring nothing. [[a-poisoned-binary-can-lose-a-path-race-to-the-real-one]]
            return got, open(rec).read()

        got, m = said(-15, "ran")
        check("a signal-killed fire is reported as KILLED, not as an exit of its own",
              got == 1 and "killed by signal 15" in m and "exited" not in m)
        got, m = said(124, "timeout")
        check("a timed-out fire names the bound that cut it off",
              got == 1 and "FIRE_TIMEOUT" in m)
        got, m = said(127, "exec:No such file")
        check("a fire that never executed says so, rather than reporting an exit code",
              got == 1 and "could not be executed" in m)
        got, m = said(2, "ran")
        check("rc=2 is tagged as the mesh's n/a convention, not reported as a plain fault",
              got == 1 and "n/a convention" in m and "DECLINE" in m)
        got, m = said(3, "ran")
        check("a line that failed on its own reports its own exit code",
              got == 1 and "exited 3 on its own" in m and "killed" not in m)
        # THE LOAD-BEARING SENTENCE. at-most-once means this tool can never re-run the slot, so the
        # alert that does not say a HAND is the only re-runner leaves the reader expecting a retry
        # that is never coming — worse than silence, because it manufactures false comfort.
        every = []
        for _rc, _how in ((-15, "ran"), (124, "timeout"), (127, "exec:x"), (3, "ran")):
            every.append(said(_rc, _how)[1])
        check("EVERY failure wording states the slot is closed and a hand is the only re-runner",
              all("will not be retried" in m.lower() and "is the only re-runner" in m.lower()
                  for m in every))
        check("every failure wording names the tool and the slot",
              all("mesh-job-apply" in m and "2026-08-26T07:23:00Z" in m for m in every))

        # A DEAD ALERTER MUST READ AS A DEAD ALERTER. If mesh-chat is absent or refuses, the return
        # must be -1 so the ledger records alerted=FAILED. Reporting success here would rebuild the
        # exact defect this closes, one ring out. [[a-gates-verdict-with-no-reader]]
        os.environ["PATH"] = os.path.join(_ad, "nothing-here")
        check("an unreachable board renders the alert FAILED, never delivered",
              alert_failed_fire("mesh-job-apply", "s", -15, "ran") == -1)
        with open(os.path.join(_ad, "mesh-chat"), "w") as f:
            f.write("#!/bin/sh\nexit 1\n")
        os.chmod(os.path.join(_ad, "mesh-chat"), 0o755)
        os.environ["PATH"] = _ad + os.pathsep + _keep_path
        check("a board that REFUSES the post renders the alert FAILED, not delivered",
              alert_failed_fire("mesh-job-apply", "s", -15, "ran") == -1)
    finally:
        os.environ["PATH"] = _keep_path
        shutil.rmtree(_ad, ignore_errors=True)

    # WIRED IS A SEPARATE CLAIM FROM CORRECT. [[passing-a-test-and-being-wired-are-unrelated-facts]]
    # Measured while writing this suite: with every arm above green, a mutant deleting the
    # alert_failed_fire CALL from the fire loop — i.e. restoring the exact defect this closes — passed,
    # and so did one emptying the renderer's loop over failed_slots(). Testing the parts and not the
    # wiring is the same failure as a verdict with no reader, one ring out. So the pass is driven END
    # TO END here: a real subprocess, a fake HOME, a stubbed crontab/journalctl/mesh-chat, and a line
    # that really does exit non-zero.
    _ed = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-e2e-")
    try:
        _bin = os.path.join(_ed, "bin"); _home = os.path.join(_ed, "home")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(_home, ".mesh")); os.makedirs(_bin)
        _said = os.path.join(_ed, "said")
        _slot = time.time() - 3600.0                      # an hour ago, inside the window
        _st = time.gmtime(_slot)
        # The node must read as DOWN across the slot, or the verdict is `covered` and nothing fires.
        # Two boots with a hole around the slot, and BOOT_GRACE respected on the live one.
        _boots = json.dumps([
            {"index": -1, "first_entry": int((_slot - 7200) * 1e6), "last_entry": int((_slot - 1800) * 1e6)},
            {"index": 0,  "first_entry": int((_slot + 600) * 1e6),  "last_entry": int((time.time() + 60) * 1e6)}])
        _line = "%d %d * * * %s/failing-line >> /tmp/e2e.log 2>&1\n" % (_st.tm_min, _st.tm_hour, _bin)
        for _n, _body in (
                ("crontab",    '#!/bin/sh\ncat <<"EOF"\n' + _line + 'EOF\n'),
                ("journalctl", "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n" + _boots + "\nEOF\n"),
                ("mesh-chat",  '#!/bin/sh\nprintf "%s\\n" "$1" >> ' + _said + "\nexit 0\n"),
                ("failing-line", "#!/bin/sh\nexit 7\n")):
            with open(os.path.join(_bin, _n), "w") as f:
                f.write(_body)
            os.chmod(os.path.join(_bin, _n), 0o755)
        _env = {"HOME": _home, "PATH": _bin + os.pathsep + "/usr/bin:/bin",
                "LANG": "C.UTF-8", "MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT": "30"}
        _r = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__)],
                            env=_env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        _posted = open(_said).read() if os.path.exists(_said) else ""
        _ledger = open(os.path.join(_home, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.log")).read() \
            if os.path.exists(os.path.join(_home, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.log")) else ""
        check("END TO END: a real pass whose fire exits non-zero REACHES THE BOARD",
              "CATCH-UP FIRE FAILED" in _posted and "exited 7 on its own" in _posted)
        check("END TO END: the ledger records that the alert actually went out",
              "alerted=yes" in _ledger and "rc=7" in _ledger)
        # ...and the SAME pass, re-rendered, must now show the failure to a reader.
        _r2 = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--report"],
                             env=_env, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        check("END TO END: --report renders the failed slot it used to hide",
              "failed (fire returned rc=7" in _r2.stdout and "only re-runner" in _r2.stdout)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_ed, ignore_errors=True)

    # --- A FIRE MUST NOT SHARE THE PASS'S FATE ---------------------------------------------------
    # The cursor is claimed BEFORE the fire, so a fire that dies has SPENT a slot nothing re-runs.
    # Until 2026-08-26 the child ran in the CALLER's process group, so a Ctrl-C in a pane, an agent
    # tool-call timeout or any `timeout` up the chain reached the fire too — measured that day at
    # slot 07:23 (mesh-job-apply, rc=-15 at 46s of a 455-980s pass, the day short 13 applications).
    _fd = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-pgid-")
    try:
        _out = os.path.join(_fd, "pgid")
        # $$ inside `sh -c` is that shell's own pid; field 5 of its stat is its process GROUP.
        _rc, _how = fire(Line(*([None] * 5 + [
            "cut -d' ' -f5 /proc/$$/stat > %s" % _out, ""])), False)
        _got = open(_out).read().strip() if os.path.exists(_out) else ""
        check("a fire runs in its OWN process group, never the caller's",
              _rc == 0 and _how == "ran" and _got.isdigit() and int(_got) != os.getpgrp())
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_fd, ignore_errors=True)

    # DETACHMENT MUST NOT BUY IMMORTALITY. start_new_session moves the fire out of our group, which
    # is also the reason `p.kill()` is no longer enough: it would reap the `sh` and leave every
    # grandchild orphaned and running. So the timeout leg is asserted on a GRANDCHILD, not on the
    # shell — a version that killed only the direct child would pass a shell-only assertion while
    # leaking the browser batch the cron line actually spawned.
    # [[a-foreground-child-defers-the-signal-trap-that-would-reap-it]]
    _td = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-bound-")
    _keep_to = FIRE_TIMEOUT
    try:
        _gpid = os.path.join(_td, "grandchild.pid")
        FIRE_TIMEOUT = 2
        _t0 = time.time()
        # `sleep 60 &` is a GRANDCHILD of the fire: it survives a kill aimed at the shell alone.
        _rc, _how = fire(Line(*([None] * 5 + [
            "sleep 60 & echo $! > %s; wait" % _gpid, ""])), False)
        _el = time.time() - _t0
        _gone = True
        try:
            _gp = int(open(_gpid).read().strip())
            for _i in range(40):
                try:
                    os.kill(_gp, 0)
                except OSError:
                    break
                time.sleep(0.25)
            else:
                _gone = False
                os.kill(_gp, signal.SIGKILL)
        except (IOError, ValueError):
            _gone = False
        check("FIRE_TIMEOUT still binds after the fire was detached",
              _rc == 124 and _how == "timeout" and _el < 30)
        check("the timeout reaps the fire's GROUP, so no grandchild outlives the bound", _gone)
    finally:
        FIRE_TIMEOUT = _keep_to
        shutil.rmtree(_td, ignore_errors=True)

    # THE BEHAVIOURAL STRAND. The arm above asserts the mechanism; this one drives the FAULT — a
    # real pass, signalled the way the pane signalled it, and the artifact is whether the work the
    # spent slot stood for actually happened. A mechanism assertion alone would go green for a
    # detachment that some later `timeout` re-attached one layer up.
    # [[a-behavioural-strand-drill-beats-the-scan-that-named-the-bug]]
    _kd = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-strand-")
    try:
        _kbin = os.path.join(_kd, "bin"); _khome = os.path.join(_kd, "home")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(_khome, ".mesh")); os.makedirs(_kbin)
        _kmark = os.path.join(_kd, "the-work-happened")
        _kslot = time.time() - 3600.0
        _kst = time.gmtime(_kslot)
        _kboots = json.dumps([
            {"index": -1, "first_entry": int((_kslot - 7200) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((_kslot - 1800) * 1e6)},
            {"index": 0, "first_entry": int((_kslot + 600) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((time.time() + 60) * 1e6)}])
        _kline = "%d %d * * * %s/slow-line >> /dev/null 2>&1\n" % (_kst.tm_min, _kst.tm_hour, _kbin)
        for _n, _body in (
                ("crontab",    '#!/bin/sh\ncat <<"EOF"\n' + _kline + 'EOF\n'),
                ("journalctl", "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n" + _kboots + "\nEOF\n"),
                ("mesh-chat",  "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n"),
                # Long enough that the TERM lands while it is genuinely mid-fire, short enough
                # that the suite stays cheap.
                ("slow-line",  "#!/bin/sh\nsleep 8\n: > %s\nexit 0\n" % _kmark)):
            with open(os.path.join(_kbin, _n), "w") as f:
                f.write(_body)
            os.chmod(os.path.join(_kbin, _n), 0o755)
        _kenv = {"HOME": _khome, "PATH": _kbin + os.pathsep + "/usr/bin:/bin",
                 "LANG": "C.UTF-8", "MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT": "60"}
        # start_new_session here is the TEST's own scaffolding: it gives the pass a group of its
        # own so the suite can signal exactly that group and nothing else — signalling our own
        # would kill the suite. [[a-name-matching-stop-kills-your-other-selves]]
        _kp = subprocess.Popen([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__)], env=_kenv,
                               stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                               start_new_session=True)
        _kstate = os.path.join(_khome, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.state")
        for _i in range(120):                     # wait until the slot is CLAIMED and firing
            if os.path.exists(_kstate) and "in-flight" in open(_kstate).read():
                break
            time.sleep(0.25)
        _claimed = os.path.exists(_kstate) and "in-flight" in open(_kstate).read()
        # A group that is already gone means the pass finished before we could signal it — the
        # drill never ran, and that must read as a FAILED arm, never as a silent pass. An
        # exception here would take the whole suite down and hide every arm after it.
        # [[a-red-gate-hides-every-gate-after-it]]
        try:
            os.killpg(_kp.pid, signal.SIGTERM)    # exactly what the pane did on 2026-08-26
            _signalled = True
        except OSError:
            _signalled = False
        try:
            _kp.wait(timeout=15)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            try:
                os.killpg(_kp.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                pass
        for _i in range(60):                      # the detached fire should still finish
            if os.path.exists(_kmark):
                break
            time.sleep(0.25)
        check("STRAND: the pass is signalled only once its slot is genuinely claimed and firing",
              _claimed and _signalled)
        check("STRAND: killing the PASS does not kill the fire whose slot it already spent",
              _claimed and _signalled and os.path.exists(_kmark))
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_kd, ignore_errors=True)

    # The renderer's predicate, called through the SAME entry point the renderer uses.
    _st = {"a": {"last_rc": "in-flight"}, "b": {"last_rc": 0}, "c": {"last_rc": -15},
           "d": {"last_rc": 124}, "e": {}}
    check("a fire that RETURNED non-zero is visible to the report (the reader that was missing)",
          [k for k, _ in failed_slots(_st)] == ["c", "d"])
    check("in-flight is NOT folded into failed — they are different facts",
          "a" not in [k for k, _ in failed_slots(_st)])
    check("a successful or never-fired slot raises nothing",
          "b" not in [k for k, _ in failed_slots(_st)] and "e" not in [k for k, _ in failed_slots(_st)])

    # --- THE CALLEE'S DECLARED BOUND -------------------------------------------------------------
    _bd = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-bound-decl-")
    try:
        def _w(name, body):
            f = os.path.join(_bd, name)
            with open(f, "w") as h:
                h.write(body)
            os.chmod(f, 0o755)
            return f

        _f1 = _w("mesh-long", "#!/bin/sh\n# max-runtime: 72000\n# catchup-truncatable: cursor per item\nexit 0\n")
        check("a declared bound and its truncatable clause are read from the callee's own source",
              declared_bound("x", _f1) == (72000, "cursor per item"))
        _f2 = _w("mesh-plain", "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n")
        check("a callee that declares nothing reads as UNDECLARED, never as a guessed default",
              declared_bound("x", _f2) == (None, None))
        check("an unreadable callee reads as UNDECLARED rather than raising or inventing a bound",
              declared_bound("x", os.path.join(_bd, "nope")) == (None, None))
        _f3 = _w("mesh-late", "#!/bin/sh\n" + ("# pad\n" * (BOUND_HEADER_LINES + 5)) +
                 "# max-runtime: 99\n")
        check("the header read is BOUNDED — a declaration past the header window is not scanned",
              declared_bound("x", _f3) == (None, None))
        # A GATE THAT ITS OWN EXPLANATORY PROSE SATISFIES IS NOT A GATE. This file documents both
        # header spellings in a comment block; if the reader matched those, this tool would declare
        # a bound about ITSELF out of its own manual.
        # [[a-source-text-gate-is-satisfied-by-its-own-explanatory-prose]]
        check("this tool's own documentation of the headers is NOT read as a declaration",
              declared_bound("x", os.path.abspath(__file__)) == (None, None))
        # THAT ARM ALONE DOES NOT TEST THE ANCHOR. This file happens to contain no digit after any
        # `max-runtime:`, so it passes identically whether the pattern is anchored or not — a
        # near-miss corpus satisfying an absence guard. A mutant that dropped the anchor stayed
        # GREEN through it. The discriminating fixture is a callee whose own COMMENTARY quotes the
        # convention WITH a real number: only a start-anchored, end-anchored whole-line match can
        # tell that apart from the declaration it describes.
        # [[a-near-miss-corpus-satisfies-an-absence-guard]]
        # The prose lines are shaped to defeat EACH anchor separately: the first ends in the number
        # (so the END anchor alone would admit it — only the START anchor rejects it), the second
        # buries it mid-line (so the START anchor alone would admit it), and the third describes the
        # truncatable clause. A single prose line would have left one anchor untested, which is how
        # the first version of this fixture let a start-anchor mutant stay green.
        _f4 = _w("mesh-prose", "#!/bin/sh\n"
                 "# a callee may declare its worst case as # max-runtime: 3600\n"
                 "# the catch-up hand reads a `# max-runtime: 3600` line, and a\n"
                 "# `# catchup-truncatable: because the cursor moves` line beside it.\n"
                 "exit 0\n")
        check("a callee that DESCRIBES the headers in prose is not read as declaring them",
              declared_bound("x", _f4) == (None, None))
        _f5 = _w("mesh-trailing", "#!/bin/sh\n# max-runtime: 3600 (but only on the slow node)\n")
        check("a declaration must be the WHOLE comment line — trailing prose is not a bound",
              declared_bound("x", _f5) == (None, None))
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_bd, ignore_errors=True)

    # --- THE OUTCOME WORD ------------------------------------------------------------------------
    check("a cut-off at OUR bound is not the same word as a failure",
          outcome_of(124, "timeout") == "cut-off" and outcome_of(3, "ran") == "failed")
    check("a cut-off the callee DECLARED safe is `truncated`, not a failure of any kind",
          outcome_of(124, "timeout", "cursor advances") == "truncated")
    check("rc=2 is DECLINED — the mesh's n/a — and never shares a word with a fault",
          outcome_of(2, "ran") == "declined")
    check("a signal kill and an exec failure keep their own words",
          outcome_of(-15, "ran") == "killed" and outcome_of(127, "exec:x") == "exec-error")
    check("rc=0 is ok whatever the declaration says",
          outcome_of(0, "ran") == "ok" and outcome_of(0, "ran", "why") == "ok")
    # An exit of 124 the callee CHOSE is not a cut-off: only `how` can tell them apart, which is
    # exactly the collapse that minted a CLOSED verdict against a working tool.
    check("an exit the callee chose is not read as our cut-off just because the number matches",
          outcome_of(124, "ran") == "failed")
    check("a pre-outcome row still decides itself where the rc CAN — 2 is a decline either way",
          outcome_of(2, None) == "declined" and outcome_of(3, None) == "failed"
          and outcome_of(-15, None) == "killed")
    check("a pre-outcome 124 is the one rc that cannot decide itself, and says so",
          outcome_of(124, None) == "outcome-unrecorded")
    check("a truncated slot is NOT a failed slot to the report's predicate",
          [k for k, _ in failed_slots({"t": {"last_rc": 124, "last_outcome": "truncated"},
                                       "f": {"last_rc": 124, "last_outcome": "cut-off"}})] == ["f"])

    # --- END TO END: an UN-FIREABLE slot is skipped, not fired-and-closed ------------------------
    # The whole fault in one pass: a daily line whose callee declares a bound past FIRE_TIMEOUT.
    # Before this, the pass claimed the cursor, fired, was killed at the bound and closed the slot.
    _ud = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-unfireable-")
    try:
        _ub = os.path.join(_ud, "bin"); _uh = os.path.join(_ud, "home")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(_uh, ".mesh")); os.makedirs(_ub)
        _uran = os.path.join(_ud, "IT-RAN")
        _usaid = os.path.join(_ud, "said")
        _uslot = time.time() - 3600.0
        _ust = time.gmtime(_uslot)
        _uboots = json.dumps([
            {"index": -1, "first_entry": int((_uslot - 7200) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((_uslot - 1800) * 1e6)},
            {"index": 0, "first_entry": int((_uslot + 600) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((time.time() + 60) * 1e6)}])
        _uline = "%d %d * * * %s/mesh-slowline >> /tmp/u.log 2>&1\n" % (_ust.tm_min, _ust.tm_hour, _ub)
        for _n, _body in (
                ("crontab", '#!/bin/sh\ncat <<"EOF"\n' + _uline + 'EOF\n'),
                ("journalctl", "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n" + _uboots + "\nEOF\n"),
                ("mesh-chat", '#!/bin/sh\nprintf "%s\\n" "$1" >> ' + _usaid + "\nexit 0\n"),
                ("mesh-slowline", "#!/bin/sh\n# max-runtime: 72000\ntouch %s\nexit 0\n" % _uran)):
            with open(os.path.join(_ub, _n), "w") as f:
                f.write(_body)
            os.chmod(os.path.join(_ub, _n), 0o755)
        _uenv = {"HOME": _uh, "PATH": _ub + os.pathsep + "/usr/bin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8",
                 "MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT": "30"}
        _ur = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__)],
                             env=_uenv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        _ulg = open(os.path.join(_uh, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.log")).read()
        _ustate = json.load(open(os.path.join(_uh, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.state")))
        _uposted = open(_usaid).read() if os.path.exists(_usaid) else ""
        # THE artifact: the callee did not run. Not "the ledger says skipped" — the process itself.
        check("UN-FIREABLE: a line declaring a bound past FIRE_TIMEOUT is NOT fired at all",
              not os.path.exists(_uran))
        check("UN-FIREABLE: the slot is NOT spent — no cursor is claimed, so nothing is closed",
              "cursor" not in _ustate.get("mesh-slowline", {})
              and "last_rc" not in _ustate.get("mesh-slowline", {}))
        check("UN-FIREABLE: the ledger gives it its OWN verb, never a `fired` row with an rc",
              "skipped-bound" in _ulg and "declared=72000" in _ulg
              and "tool=mesh-slowline\trc=" not in _ulg)
        check("UN-FIREABLE: the board is told, and told that nothing was spent",
              "CATCH-UP SKIPPED" in _uposted and "NOTHING WAS SPENT" in _uposted
              and "max-runtime=72000" in _uposted)
        check("UN-FIREABLE: --report names the mismatch instead of hiding an unfired slot",
              "bound-mismatch" in subprocess.run(
                  [sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--report"], env=_uenv,
                  capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120).stdout)
        # A REPEAT PASS MUST NOT RE-POST. The slot stays missed until it ages out, so an
        # unthrottled alert would put one line per pass on the board for a whole window.
        _n1 = _uposted.count("CATCH-UP SKIPPED")
        subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__)], env=_uenv,
                       capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        _n2 = open(_usaid).read().count("CATCH-UP SKIPPED") if os.path.exists(_usaid) else 0
        check("UN-FIREABLE: the board line is throttled to one per slot, not one per pass",
              _n2 == _n1 == 1)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_ud, ignore_errors=True)

    # --- END TO END: a DECLARED-TRUNCATABLE callee is fired and its cut-off is not a failure -----
    _td2 = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-trunc-")
    try:
        _tb = os.path.join(_td2, "bin"); _th = os.path.join(_td2, "home")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(_th, ".mesh")); os.makedirs(_tb)
        _tstart = os.path.join(_td2, "STARTED")
        _tsaid = os.path.join(_td2, "said")
        _tslot = time.time() - 3600.0
        _tst = time.gmtime(_tslot)
        _tboots = json.dumps([
            {"index": -1, "first_entry": int((_tslot - 7200) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((_tslot - 1800) * 1e6)},
            {"index": 0, "first_entry": int((_tslot + 600) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((time.time() + 60) * 1e6)}])
        _tline = "%d %d * * * %s/mesh-resumable >> /tmp/t.log 2>&1\n" % (_tst.tm_min, _tst.tm_hour, _tb)
        for _n, _body in (
                ("crontab", '#!/bin/sh\ncat <<"EOF"\n' + _tline + 'EOF\n'),
                ("journalctl", "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n" + _tboots + "\nEOF\n"),
                ("mesh-chat", '#!/bin/sh\nprintf "%s\\n" "$1" >> ' + _tsaid + "\nexit 0\n"),
                ("mesh-resumable", "#!/bin/sh\n# max-runtime: 72000\n"
                                   "# catchup-truncatable: a cursor advances after every item\n"
                                   "touch %s\nsleep 60\n" % _tstart)):
            with open(os.path.join(_tb, _n), "w") as f:
                f.write(_body)
            os.chmod(os.path.join(_tb, _n), 0o755)
        _tenv = {"HOME": _th, "PATH": _tb + os.pathsep + "/usr/bin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8",
                 "MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT": "2"}
        subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__)],
                       env=_tenv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        _tlg = open(os.path.join(_th, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.log")).read()
        _tstate = json.load(open(os.path.join(_th, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.state")))
        _tposted = open(_tsaid).read() if os.path.exists(_tsaid) else ""
        check("TRUNCATABLE: a callee that declares a cut safe IS fired, not skipped",
              os.path.exists(_tstart) and "skipped-bound" not in _tlg)
        check("TRUNCATABLE: the cut-off is recorded as `truncated`, not as a failure",
              "outcome=truncated" in _tlg
              and _tstate["mesh-resumable"]["last_outcome"] == "truncated")
        check("TRUNCATABLE: no FAILED board line is raised against a tool that did what it declared",
              "CATCH-UP FIRE FAILED" not in _tposted)
        _trep = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--report"], env=_tenv,
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120).stdout
        check("TRUNCATABLE: --report shows the truncation and does NOT summon a hand for it",
              "truncated (fire cut at" in _trep
              and "a hand is the only re-runner): mesh-resumable" not in _trep)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_td2, ignore_errors=True)

    # --- A DECLINE AND A FAILURE ARE TWO WORDS WITH OPPOSITE REMEDIES ---------------------------
    # Six lines on this node read as CLOSED-as-failed on 2026-08-29 and four had exited 2 — the
    # mesh's own n/a — i.e. they stood down honestly and were filed as broken.
    _dd = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-declined-")
    try:
        _db = os.path.join(_dd, "bin"); _dh = os.path.join(_dd, "home")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(_dh, ".mesh")); os.makedirs(_db)
        _dslot = time.time() - 3600.0
        _dst = time.gmtime(_dslot)
        _dboots = json.dumps([
            {"index": -1, "first_entry": int((_dslot - 7200) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((_dslot - 1800) * 1e6)},
            {"index": 0, "first_entry": int((_dslot + 600) * 1e6),
             "last_entry": int((time.time() + 60) * 1e6)}])
        _dline = "%d %d * * * %s/mesh-absent-organ >> /tmp/d.log 2>&1\n" % (_dst.tm_min, _dst.tm_hour, _db)
        for _n, _body in (
                ("crontab", '#!/bin/sh\ncat <<"EOF"\n' + _dline + 'EOF\n'),
                ("journalctl", "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n" + _dboots + "\nEOF\n"),
                ("mesh-chat", "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n"),
                ("mesh-absent-organ", "#!/bin/sh\nexit 2\n")):
            with open(os.path.join(_db, _n), "w") as f:
                f.write(_body)
            os.chmod(os.path.join(_db, _n), 0o755)
        _denv = {"HOME": _dh, "PATH": _db + os.pathsep + "/usr/bin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8",
                 "MESH_CATCHUP_TIMEOUT": "30"}
        subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__)],
                       env=_denv, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120)
        _dlg = open(os.path.join(_dh, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.log")).read()
        _drep = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--report"], env=_denv,
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120).stdout
        check("DECLINED: the ledger row carries the word, not just the rc a reader must decode",
              "outcome=declined" in _dlg and "rc=2" in _dlg)
        check("DECLINED: --report says declined and does NOT summon a hand to re-run it",
              "declined (fire exited 2" in _drep
              and "a hand is the only re-runner): mesh-absent-organ" not in _drep)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_dd, ignore_errors=True)

    # --- THE REPORT READS THE RECORDED WORD, IT DOES NOT RE-DERIVE IT ---------------------------
    # A renderer that re-classifies from `last_rc` alone has strictly LESS evidence than the pass
    # that wrote the row -- it has neither `how` nor the callee's declaration -- so it prints a
    # different verdict for the same row and two readers disagree about one fact. The case that
    # separates them is the plain cut-off: recorded `cut-off` at the time, but re-derived from rc
    # alone it can only be `outcome-unrecorded`, because rc=124 genuinely cannot decide itself.
    # Found by a mutant that swapped the read for a re-derive and stayed GREEN through 69 arms.
    _rd = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="cron-catchup-render-")
    try:
        _rb = os.path.join(_rd, "bin"); _rh = os.path.join(_rd, "home")
        os.makedirs(os.path.join(_rh, ".mesh")); os.makedirs(_rb)
        _rslot = time.time() - 3600.0
        _rst = time.gmtime(_rslot)
        # Node UP across the whole window, so the slot is covered and nothing fires: this arm is
        # about the RENDER of an already-recorded row, and a fire here would be a second variable.
        _rboots = json.dumps([{"index": 0, "first_entry": int((_rslot - 7200) * 1e6),
                               "last_entry": int((time.time() + 60) * 1e6)}])
        _rline = "%d %d * * * %s/mesh-cut >> /tmp/c.log 2>&1\n" % (_rst.tm_min, _rst.tm_hour, _rb)
        for _n, _body in (
                ("crontab", '#!/bin/sh\ncat <<"EOF"\n' + _rline + 'EOF\n'),
                ("journalctl", "#!/bin/sh\ncat <<'EOF'\n" + _rboots + "\nEOF\n"),
                ("mesh-chat", "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n"),
                ("mesh-cut", "#!/bin/sh\nexit 0\n")):
            with open(os.path.join(_rb, _n), "w") as f:
                f.write(_body)
            os.chmod(os.path.join(_rb, _n), 0o755)
        # A row the pass recorded as a plain cut-off. Re-derived from rc alone it could only be
        # `outcome-unrecorded`, so the two readings are distinguishable in the rendered text.
        with open(os.path.join(_rh, ".mesh", "cron-catchup.state"), "w") as f:
            json.dump({"mesh-cut": {"cursor": int(_rslot), "last_fire": "2026-08-29T04:41:00Z",
                                    "last_rc": 124, "last_how": "timeout",
                                    "last_outcome": "cut-off"}}, f)
        _renv = {"HOME": _rh, "PATH": _rb + os.pathsep + "/usr/bin:/bin", "LANG": "C.UTF-8"}
        _rrep = subprocess.run([sys.executable, os.path.abspath(__file__), "--report"], env=_renv,
                               capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=120).stdout
        check("the report prints the word the PASS recorded, never one re-derived from rc alone",
              "(cut-off)" in _rrep and "outcome-unrecorded" not in _rrep)
    finally:
        shutil.rmtree(_rd, ignore_errors=True)

    print("\n%s: %d check(s), %d failure(s)" %
          ("FAIL" if fails else "OK", check.n, len(fails)))
    return 1 if fails else 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
